WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:06.560
Take a moment to think about the appearance&nbsp;
of a modern bird – like a parrot, or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:12.560
similar. We know these animals have relatively&nbsp;
thick bodies, feathery coats, unique patterns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.160
and varying colors. Now, let’s look at a&nbsp;
parrot’s skeleton. You’ll notice that based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.200
on the bones alone, these birds seem like&nbsp;
they’d have long, slender frames — yet the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.680
layers of skin and feather coverings make&nbsp;
them look quite different than their gaunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.440
skeletal structures might suggest. And this exact&nbsp;
phenomenon presents a practical challenge when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:39.280
comes to reconstructing accurate depictions of&nbsp;
dinosaurs. An increasing number of soft tissue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.640
discoveries have suggested that many (though not&nbsp;
all) species of dinosaurs possessed more feathers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.240
layers of fat, and other unusual details than one&nbsp;
might assume based on the outdated depictions many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.280
of us grew up with. The challenge of capturing&nbsp;
the forgotten peculiarities of dinosaurs is at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:01.040
the center of “All Yesterdays,” a book written and&nbsp;
illustrated in part by C.M. Koseman — whose work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.640
you may recognize from his other most famous book&nbsp;
“All Tomorrows.” The 2012 speculative paleo-art&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.920
book All Yesterdays was created not just by&nbsp;
Kosemen, however, but also Darren Naish — an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:18.400
expert paleontologist and author, and John Conway:&nbsp;
a skilled paleo-artist. These three are brilliant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.160
and I have links to where you can follow and&nbsp;
support them in this video’s description.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.360
Not long ago, I covered a section of this book&nbsp;
titled “All Todays,” in which the authors imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.400
what might happen if, millions of years into&nbsp;
the future, clueless alien scientists tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.000
to create depictions of long extinct organisms&nbsp;
based solely on the fossil record — with pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.360
hilarious results. But I can’t emphasize enough&nbsp;
that, despite misconceptions to the contrary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.800
All Yesterdays isn’t trying to belittle the field&nbsp;
of paleontology with these images — or suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.280
modern scientists are similarly clueless&nbsp;
when it comes to reconstructing dinosaurs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.240
Paleoart has come a long way, thanks in part&nbsp;
to the efforts of these creators. The three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.200
authors — all of whom are deeply involved in&nbsp;
the paleontology community themselves — make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.840
it clear that modern paleontologists aren’t&nbsp;
just blindly interpreting fossils — but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.960
making great strides in integrating studies of&nbsp;
feather impressions and soft tissue preservation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.520
to gain complete pictures of what these incredible&nbsp;
creatures might have looked like. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.240
there are still, of course, a diversity of&nbsp;
fascinating unknowns when it comes to dinosaurs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.120
and All Yesterdays explores how speculation&nbsp;
— as long as it’s cautiously grounded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.720
science — can both help and even play a vital&nbsp;
role in showcasing the intriguing possibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.080
of how dinosaurs might have looked and behaved.&nbsp;
All Yesterdays stresses a balance between caution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.800
and speculation, always pointing out that&nbsp;
many of the reconstructions within the pages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:49.680
might be completely off the mark. That’s just&nbsp;
the nature of speculation. With that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:56.000
let’s get started, and imagine what unique aspects&nbsp;
of dinosaurs may not show up in the fossil record.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.960
The first out-of-the-norm reconstruction of&nbsp;
a dinosaur All Yesterdays explores is of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.720
Carnotaurus, drawn at a unique angle by John&nbsp;
Conway. While the bull-horned predators are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.320
usually depicted from the side, here, the authors&nbsp;
depict the animal head on — drawing attention to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:18.160
just how strange the animal looks — with its arms&nbsp;
stuck out to the sides in a bizarre display. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.040
idea behind the strange spectacle comes from the&nbsp;
animal’s skeleton, which shows the animal had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.320
stunted, ineffectual arms — yet possessed unique&nbsp;
shoulders which would have allowed the animals to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.680
spread these arms out sideways in a way utterly&nbsp;
dissimilar to other large predatory dinosaurs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.240
and seemed to serve no practical purpose. So,&nbsp;
the authors imagine the arms on Carnotaurus (and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:46.000
the related Majungasaurus, pictured here in C.M.&nbsp;
Koseman’s one of a kind style) were waved about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:52.080
in mating or territorial displays, potentially&nbsp;
alongside bright colors — not unlike many birds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:57.520
today. A bizarre concept to be sure, but not&nbsp;
altogether improbable given the available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.960
paleontological data. Of course, dinosaur colors&nbsp;
might have also gone in the opposite direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.000
Can you see the Majungasaurs in this image?&nbsp;
How about now? Here, the authors explore the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.640
possibility that some dinosaurs might have&nbsp;
had appearances which supported camouflage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.560
and mimicry: bending into the natural features of&nbsp;
the environment much like a modern stick-bug. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:24.560
it’s usually smaller animals, not predators like&nbsp;
Majungasaurs, which employ camouflage. But some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.440
large modern predators’ body patterns, like the&nbsp;
tiger’s distinctive stripes which allow them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:35.920
blend into tall grasses, do help them ambush prey.&nbsp;
So, it’s possible certain dinosaurs might have had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:41.680
similar camouflaged appearances. Here’s another&nbsp;
recreation which, at first, might be difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.360
see. This is All Yesterday’s speculative concept&nbsp;
for a Plesiosauria, portraying the animal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:53.200
impressive camouflage. Strange though this might&nbsp;
seem to anyone familiar with traditional portrays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:59.120
of Plesiosauria, the modern oceans are swarming&nbsp;
with remarkable forms of mimicry. Just look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:04.480
incredible appearance of something like a leafy&nbsp;
seadragon, and you’ll start to understand just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.600
incredible past ocean life might have looked. Now,&nbsp;
the authors aren’t actually making a scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.400
assertion that this was what all Plesiosauria&nbsp;
(which for the record, weren’t actually dinosaurs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.760
but adjacent marine reptiles) looked like.&nbsp;
Most Plesiosauria likely had smooth skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.840
for better hydrodynamics, but it’s not impossible&nbsp;
some branches tried an ambush approach which may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:31.280
have favored better camouflage. An interesting&nbsp;
possibility for sure. Back on land, here’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:37.520
quite heavily feathered speculative reinvention&nbsp;
of a Therizinosaurus. The Therizinosaurus looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.960
quite menacing based on its skeleton alone,&nbsp;
thanks to the animal’s absurdly long claws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.240
which in the past excited artists and caused them&nbsp;
to make an overly-deadly looking animal — despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.880
the fact the animal wasn’t a predator. The thick&nbsp;
layer of feathers certainly makes the animal look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:57.200
less frightening, and quite different from&nbsp;
their skeletal appearance. If you compare the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.560
gaunt skeleton of say, an owl, however, to the&nbsp;
quite different shape of the actual bird, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:08.080
could start to see how such a difference between&nbsp;
skeleton and body size isn’t too crazy a concept.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:14.560
Therizinosaurus skeletons also have wide hips, so&nbsp;
it’s been theorized they have sizeable physiques.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.440
Beyond proto-feathers, All Yesterdays also&nbsp;
explores the hypothetical that some dinosaurs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.800
like Heterodontosaurus, might have had defensive,&nbsp;
quill like structures — not unlike the defense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:30.000
the modern porcupine. Seem hard to believe?&nbsp;
Well, a recent exceptionally well-preserved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.600
specimen of the early ceratopsian Psittacosaurus&nbsp;
was found with bristle-like quills on its tail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.480
The Psittacosaurus is actually one of the most&nbsp;
completely reconstructed dinosaurs, and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:46.480
appearance is certainly not tame. Paleontologists&nbsp;
don’t think the bristles on its tail were for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:52.000
self-defense, but it’s certainly possible some&nbsp;
geneses like Heterodontosaurus — which we also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.960
now know had some kind of spike-like dermal quills&nbsp;
— could have used their bristles for self-defense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.560
Going further into hypothetical territory&nbsp;
regarding proto-feathers, the authors imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:09.120
a group of Leaellynasaura Amicagraphica as rotund&nbsp;
furballs quite different from the popular notion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:15.040
of a ‘dinosaur.’ Far from fanciful speculation,&nbsp;
however, the authors’ recreation is based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:20.000
evidence that the Leaellynasaura lived in a&nbsp;
near-polar region, and almost certainly had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:25.120
to endure sub-zero temperatures. While one might&nbsp;
look at such a reconstruction and think it looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:31.120
too adorable for a dinosaur, The Authors point&nbsp;
out the hesitancy around depicting dinosaurs as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.920
‘cute’ is a potentially an overly-limiting&nbsp;
one. Also incorporated into this design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.840
Leaellynasaura tails were almost three times as&nbsp;
long as their body, for reasons paleontologists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:47.520
aren’t quite certain of. All Yesterdays depicts&nbsp;
their tails as strange, flagpole-like tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:53.040
for social displays. While easy to dismiss this&nbsp;
concept as overly-speculative, the authors point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:58.160
out that while it’s certainly unlikely, it’s only&nbsp;
so much weirder than what you might see anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:03.920
else in nature. But appearance is only one half of&nbsp;
the speculative puzzle when it comes to dinosaurs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:09.360
The authors observe that various unique behaviors&nbsp;
likely weren’t preserved in the fossil record.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:14.160
Just think about the mating dances of birds&nbsp;
of paradise, the unique calls of whales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:20.960
or even something as simple as the dung rolling&nbsp;
of Dung Beetles. All of these behaviors are huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.000
parts of what gives these animals their identity,&nbsp;
and yet most of them could never be understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:30.560
from remains alone. And it stands to reason&nbsp;
some dinosaurs might have had behaviors which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:36.560
fall into the same category. Here’s an intriguing&nbsp;
speculative example of the Elasmosaurus, marine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:42.560
reptiles which aren’t actually dinosaurs, behaving&nbsp;
in a highly unusual manner. Here, the authors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:48.320
invent a scenario where the Elasmosaurus lunge&nbsp;
out of the depths and wave their necks — competing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:53.600
to prove who is the strongest. Older art of&nbsp;
Elasmosaurus sometimes shows them holding their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:59.200
necks above the water’s surface with ease, but&nbsp;
modern paleontology has proven these animals had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.480
heavy bones and dense necks, which could only be&nbsp;
lifted out of the water with significant effort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:10.640
So, the authors imagine these animals exerting&nbsp;
such strain as a form of social competition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:16.560
Of course, this is absolutely and unapologetically&nbsp;
speculative, yet equivalently remarkable behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:22.400
is likely lost somewhere in our past. In a similar&nbsp;
vein of speculative thought, here the authors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:28.480
imagine Protoceratops engaging in a behavior&nbsp;
there’s no direct evidence for in the slightest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:33.600
And yet, certain breeds of goats also climb&nbsp;
trees, a strange-looking behavior you wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.358
guess the hooved mammals would engage in based on&nbsp;
its body plan alone. It’s something like this sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.358 --> 00:09:39.360
of unexpected conduct which might be absent in the&nbsp;
fossil record. (and yes, these modern goats? They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:50.240
are not a hoax—they truly hang out in trees.) On&nbsp;
the subject of dinosaurs doing things they’re not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.840
known for, playing is certainly a significant&nbsp;
example. Instead of showing a dinosaur striking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:01.120
a pose or getting attacked by a predator, this&nbsp;
recreation shows a Carmarasaurus grandis enjoying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:07.120
a simple roll in the mud. Speculative though this&nbsp;
might seem, almost all living animals engage in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:13.280
some form of play —both larger mammals, but also&nbsp;
various species of birds, reptiles, and even fish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:21.360
take part in behavior for no other practical&nbsp;
reason that it seems to be fun. So dinosaurs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.160
even if they’re sometimes thought of creatures&nbsp;
that lived in constant battle with each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:33.520
almost certainly engaged in play too. And here at&nbsp;
the end is an image which, despite its simplicity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:40.880
is one of the hardest to process. It’s showing a&nbsp;
t-rex just sleeping — but so much of our classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:46.080
perception of the creature is of a roaring&nbsp;
monster with its jaws agape ready to strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:51.600
that we forget the tyrannosaurus is also…&nbsp;
just an animal. And animals sleep — especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:56.720
hunting animals, who need to conserve energy and&nbsp;
therefore spend a considerable amount of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:03.360
resting. It’s not certain if T rex’s slept&nbsp;
on their sides or not — but it’s still an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.560
intriguing image that gets the viewer to think of&nbsp;
the animal as more than just a machine of death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.600
Ultimately, science and speculation aren’t&nbsp;
concepts in opposition to each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:19.680
All paleontological art of long-extinct creatures&nbsp;
is speculative to some degree, and thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:25.120
interesting — albeit unusual — possibilities,&nbsp;
can help us appreciate all we know — and don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:30.800
know — about these remarkable animals from the&nbsp;
distant past. And that’s the end of our glimpse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:36.000
at the intriguing “All Yesterdays.” If you liked&nbsp;
this content, please consider supporting Kosemen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:40.080
Naish, and Conway’s work by purchasing&nbsp;
a copy of the book — and by patronizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.440
their other works and social media, which as&nbsp;
always you can find in the links below. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:50.640
thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,&nbsp;
please lend your support and like, subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.640 --> 00:12:09.840
and hit the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

